using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.RazorPages;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class PostModel(AppDbContext db) : PageModel
{
    public Post Post { get; private set; } = default!;

    public async Task<IActionResult> OnGetAsync(string slug)
    {
        var post = await db.Posts.FirstOrDefaultAsync(p => p.Slug == slug);
        if (post is null) return NotFound(); // sg-probe-404 and every unknown slug: real 404, empty body, then the status page
        Post = post;
        return Page();
    }
}

ASP.NET Core 9/10 Razor Pages. A plain HTML form that works without JavaScript: Razor Pages validate the antiforgery token automatically and the form tag helper emits it (SEC-024; keep it, and never call DisableAntiforgery()). If you build the form as a minimal API with [FromForm] instead, call builder.Services.AddAntiforgery() and app.UseAntiforgery() after authentication/authorization: form-bound minimal APIs then require the token. DataAnnotations validate on the server; delivery goes to your webhook (FORM_WEBHOOK_URL: CRM, form provider or mail API) through IHttpClientFactory with an 8s timeout; the thank-you redirect (303) is sent only after a 2xx answer, otherwise 502 (rendered by the error page from the error-pages recipe). The fixed-window limiter allows 5 POSTs per client per 10 minutes and answers 429; it is per instance (use a distributed limiter when you run several) and behind a proxy needs the Forwarded Headers middleware so RemoteIpAddress is the client. Add Cloudflare Turnstile (cf-turnstile-response, verified server-side) if bots get past the honeypot.
