ASP.NET Core 9/10 health checks. MapHealthChecks answers 200 for Healthy and Degraded and 503 for Unhealthy by default (HealthCheckOptions.ResultStatusCodes); the default body is the bare word "Healthy"/"Unhealthy", so the ResponseWriter below writes the tiny JSON instead and never prints entries, descriptions or exceptions. With AllowCachingResponses left at its default (false) the middleware sets Cache-Control, Expires and Pragma to prevent caching. AddDbContextCheck (package Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks.EntityFrameworkCore) calls EF Core's CanConnectAsync, so a down database is Unhealthy -> 503. Bound the wait with the connection string's own timeout (SQL Server `Connect Timeout=2`, Npgsql `Timeout=2`), because the check has no timeout of its own. No database: replace AddDbContextCheck with a check of your critical dependency (the placeholder below always passes).
### file: Program.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics.HealthChecks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks;

builder.Services.AddHealthChecks()
    .AddDbContextCheck<AppDbContext>(); // your DbContext; CanConnectAsync against the real database
    // no database: .AddCheck("critical-dependency", () => HealthCheckResult.Healthy())  <- wire to your dependency; failure must be Unhealthy

// ... after UseRouting():
app.MapHealthChecks("/healthz", new HealthCheckOptions
{
    // Healthy and Degraded keep the default 200; Unhealthy keeps the default 503 (OPS-002)
    ResponseWriter = (context, report) =>
    {
        context.Response.ContentType = "application/json";
        // no versions, engine names, hostnames or exception text in the body
        return context.Response.WriteAsync(report.Status == HealthStatus.Unhealthy
            ? "{\"status\":\"unavailable\"}"
            : "{\"status\":\"ok\"}");
    },
});
